module contract;

// Monkey diagnostic logging — resource-projection vocabulary version 1.
//
// Card W36-CORPUS-CONTRACTS-3. ONE TRUSTED ASSUMPTION about one upstream
// function. This module verifies nothing: the C body is not analyzed by this
// loader, and a declaration is a premise. Any verdict that rests on it is a
// verdict RELATIVE TO this declaration, never a proof of the C implementation.
//
// Read from the pinned checkout (`build/cycle19/fluent-bit-clang-source`) at
// Fluent Bit 66910c10a4d7eafa810b84229cf2dcf7b0f26f97 on this card's own run.
//
// This is this card's ONE variadic declaration, and it is the
// `fluent_bit/logging.qcore` shape one library over: the same
// `resource_variadic_data_tail()` clause, on the same reading, for the same
// reason. That module (card PROD-CONTRACTS, clause T5 of card O04-TRACE's
// vocabulary) is the precedent every sentence below follows.
//
// ## What the call sites actually reach — measured, not assumed
//
// Unlike Fluent Bit's `flb_error`/`flb_warn`, Monkey's `mk_print` IS a real
// function and is what the analysed statements name. The wrappers around it are
// macros (`lib/monkey/include/monkey/mk_core/mk_macros.h:40-42`)
//
//     #define mk_info(...)  mk_print(MK_INFO, __VA_ARGS__)
//     #define mk_err(...)   mk_print(MK_ERR, __VA_ARGS__)
//     #define mk_warn(...)  mk_print(MK_WARN, __VA_ARGS__)
//
// so the preprocessed corpus names `mk_print` and never the three macros —
// declaring `mk_info` would be inert text, and none of the three is declared.
//
// ## Why this callee
//
// Three projected functions in the baseline are blocked by `mk_print` and by
// nothing else: `mk_config_error`
// (`lib/monkey/mk_core/mk_rconf.c:44` and `:45`, two leaves),
// `mk_rconf_warning` (`:51`) and `mk_plugin_event_bad_return`
// (`lib/monkey/mk_server/mk_plugin.c:709`).
//
// ## `mk_print`, read from `lib/monkey/mk_core/mk_utils.c:200-264`
//
//     void mk_print(int type, const char *format, ...)
//     {
//         time_t now;
//         struct tm *current;
//         const char *header_color = NULL;
//         ...
//         va_list args;
//
//         va_start(args, format);
//
//         switch (type) {
//         case MK_INFO: ... case MK_ERR: ... case MK_WARN: ... case MK_BUG: ...
//         }
//
//         /* Only print colors to a terminal */
//         if (!isatty(STDOUT_FILENO)) { ... }
//
//         now = time(NULL);
//         struct tm result;
//         current = localtime_r(&now, &result);
//         printf("%s[%s%i/%02i/%02i %02i:%02i:%02i%s]%s ", ...);
//         printf("%s[%s%7s%s]%s ", ...);
//
//         vprintf(format, args);
//         va_end(args);
//         printf("%s\n", reset_color);
//         fflush(stdout);
//     }
//
//   * `type` is an `int` BY VALUE. It is the `switch` selector and selects two
//     string literals. It is never dereferenced — it is not a pointer — and
//     carries no custody.
//   * `format` is a `const char *`. It reaches exactly one place,
//     `vprintf(format, args)`: it is READ, never freed, never stored, and no
//     pointer to it outlives the call. `resource_borrowed` is the faithful
//     mode.
//   * the variadic tail is consumed by `va_start`/`vprintf` and written to
//     `stdout`. What leaves this function is RENDERED TEXT on a stream, never a
//     tail argument's pointer. No tail argument is freed, and no pointer to one
//     is retained past the call. That last sentence is exactly what
//     `resource_variadic_data_tail()` declares.
//   * The local `current`/`result` pair is `localtime_r` writing into a
//     function-local `struct tm`; neither leaves.
//   * The result is `void`, so `resource_return_none()` is the faithful — and
//     the only faithful — result mode.
//
// ## What the tail clause does and does not buy
//
// `resource_variadic_data_tail()` admits NOTHING on its own. The gateway
// independently requires, PER CALL SITE, that each tail actual really is data,
// and a tail actual that may carry a tracked resource identity is refused UNDER
// this declaration exactly as it is without one.
//
// NOT CLAIMED, recorded rather than hidden: `vprintf` READS through every `%s`
// tail pointer. That is a validity/reachability requirement on the caller, not
// a custody fact, and this projection models custody only. A caller that passes
// a released pointer in a tail position is a use-after-free this declaration
// neither creates nor detects — the gateway's own liveness precondition (card
// BORROW-LIVENESS) is what refuses that, on settled denotations, unchanged here.
//
// ## ABI and modes (constraint 8)
//
// `lib/monkey/include/monkey/mk_core/mk_utils.h:49` declares
//
//     void mk_print(int type, const char *format, ...) PRINTF_WARNINGS(2,3);
//
// where `PRINTF_WARNINGS(a,b)` is
// `__attribute__ ((format (printf, a, b)))` under the pinned build and empty
// otherwise (`lib/monkey/include/monkey/mk_core/mk_macros.h:88` and `:90`). The
// attribute is a diagnostic decoration on the declaration; it changes no
// parameter type, no arity and no calling convention, and the v1 ABI projection
// reads types and pointer levels. `int` projects to `i32`, `const char *` to
// one object-pointer level `ptr<const, u8>`, and the `void` result to `unit`.
// The DECLARED arity is two, which is the fixed arity C declares; the `...` is
// carried by `resource_variadic_data_tail()` and not by a third parameter, and
// LOADER-ABI's arity gate is what keeps that honest — a tail-free contract for
// a variadic C declaration, or a declared tail for a C declaration that is not
// variadic, is a contradiction the gate reports.
//
// ## The parameter is renamed, deliberately
//
// C spells the first parameter `type`, which is a reserved word in this
// language (it is the aggregate declaration head). It is spelled `level` here.
// A declaration's parameter NAMES are its own — the ABI comparison is over
// types, pointer levels and arity — and this is the same rename
// `fluent_bit/logging.qcore` already makes for `flb_log_print`, whose C
// parameters are `type` and `fmt` and whose declaration spells them `level` and
// `format`.
//
// ## Declared although defined in a corpus unit
//
// `mk_print` is defined in `monkey-libevent/mk_core/mk_utils.c`, an audited
// unit, so it meets constraint 8's duty gate. A declared contract wins over an
// inferred W4 summary and a disagreement is a `SummaryConflict` diagnostic,
// never a silent override; a withdrawal would be scoped to `mk_utils.c` alone,
// and the three call sites this declaration exists to decide are in
// `mk_core/mk_rconf.c` and `mk_server/mk_plugin.c`, which define no body for
// it. `mk_utils.c`'s own definition stays analyzable and is not rewritten — and
// it is worth recording that that body is itself Unknown in the baseline for
// unrelated reasons (`unknown_alias` leaves at `mk_utils.c:208` and `:209`),
// which this declaration does not change and does not claim to.
//
// ## Read at the same time and DELIBERATELY LEFT UNDECLARED
//
//   * `mk_print` is also taken as a FUNCTION POINTER at
//     `lib/monkey/mk_server/mk_plugin.c:293` (`api->_error = mk_print;`). An
//     indirect call through that pointer is not a named call and no declaration
//     of any name decides it; nothing here claims otherwise.
//   * `printf`, `vprintf`, `fflush`, `isatty`, `time` and `localtime_r` are all
//     named by this body and none is declared. They are left undeclared because
//     this card selects by FUNCTION-COVER yield, and none of them is the sole
//     blocker of any projected function in the baseline.
//
// ## NOT CLAIMED
//
//   * Nothing about the logging pipeline's own resources, ordering, colour
//     selection, truncation, or output.
//   * Nothing about the tail being type-correct against the format string, or
//     about it being read at all.
//   * Nothing about thread safety — `printf`/`vprintf` interleaving across
//     threads is neither modeled nor claimed.

// ## Card CONTRACT-NORETURN-AND-BORROWED-TAILS: the borrowed tail
//
// The tail clause is now `resource_variadic_borrowed_tail()` (contract
// specification C04.2): the data tail's statement, and that every trailing
// position is `resource_borrowed`. Re-read against the body at both audited
// revisions (`lib/monkey/mk_core/mk_utils.c:199-261`, the same text at
// `66910c10` and `046accf6`; the file differs only in `mk_utils_worker_*`
// below it): the list is opened by `va_start(args, format)`, read only by
// `vprintf(format, args)`, which renders each trailing argument to `stdout`
// (C17 7.21.6.10 / 7.21.6.1: a `%s` or `%p` argument is read, a `%n`
// argument is written through as an `int *`), and closed by `va_end(args)`.
// No trailing pointer is freed, stored, returned or reachable after the
// call: each one is borrowed. The gateway reads a trailing plain name as a
// borrowed place (gateway rule 11w, T8): a live tracked pointer is admitted
// with its own obligation unchanged, and one released before the call is
// still refused (C04.2's caller-side rule). The `vprintf` reading through a
// released pointer that the NOT CLAIMED note above records is exactly that
// refusal.

// --- the variadic diagnostic entry point every Monkey logging macro calls ---
extern fn mk_print(level: i32, format: ptr<const, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(level);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(format);
    ensures normal resource_variadic_borrowed_tail();
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// ## Card W46-EXTERNAL-CALLEE-CONTRACTS: `mk_utils_libc_error`
//
// ONE TRUSTED ASSUMPTION, pinned (contract spec C06.2) after reading the body
// at both audited revisions (identical at `66910c10` and `046accf6`). It is
// the last uncontracted call of two projected functions,
// `mk_sched_send_signal` (`lib/monkey/mk_server/mk_scheduler.c:862-883`) and
// `mk_socket_create` (`lib/monkey/mk_server/mk_socket.c:120-139`); its other
// eight call sites sit in functions with other blockers.
//
// Every call site reaches it through the macro
// (`lib/monkey/include/monkey/mk_core/mk_macros.h:181`)
//
//     #define mk_libc_error(c)    mk_utils_libc_error(c, __FILE__, __LINE__)
//
// ## The body, read from `lib/monkey/include/monkey/mk_core/mk_utils.h:84-96`
//
//     #define MK_UTILS_LIBC_ERRNO_BUFFER()                       \
//         char buf[MK_UTILS_ERROR_SIZE];                         \
//         int  _err;                                             \
//         _err = errno;                                          \
//         if (strerror_r(_err, buf, MK_UTILS_ERROR_SIZE) != 0) { \
//             mk_err("strerror_r() failed");                     \
//         }
//
//     static inline void mk_utils_libc_error(char *caller, char *file, int line)
//     {
//         MK_UTILS_LIBC_ERRNO_BUFFER();
//         mk_err("%s: %s, errno=%i at %s:%i", caller, buf, _err, file, line);
//     }
//
// `mk_err` is `mk_print(MK_ERR, ...)` (declared above).
//
//   * `caller` and `file` are `char *`. Each reaches exactly one place, a `%s`
//     tail position of `mk_print`: READ, never freed, never stored, and no
//     pointer to either outlives the call. `resource_borrowed`.
//   * `line` is an `int` by value. `resource_borrowed`.
//   * `buf` is a local array and `_err` a local `int`; neither leaves.
//   * the result is `void`: `resource_return_none()`.
//
// ## Where the body lives, and what that means for checking
//
// The function is `static inline` in a header, so every unit that includes
// `mk_utils.h` holds a copy, but no analysed function of the corpus IS this
// function: the analysed units list only their own file's functions. No body
// in the corpus is therefore checked against this declaration (constraint 8,
// rule 10g); it is a trusted premise, pinned to the header's text.
//
// ## ABI (constraint 8)
//
// `char *` projects to one object-pointer level, `ptr<mut, u8>`; `int` to
// `i32`; the `void` result to `unit`. The arity is three and the C function is
// not variadic.
//
// ## NOT CLAIMED
//
//   * Nothing about the message. Which `strerror_r` the unit declares (the XSI
//     `int` form or the GNU `char *` form) decides whether `buf` is written; a
//     custody projection models custody only and says nothing about the bytes
//     `%s` reads from `buf`.
//   * Nothing about `errno` or thread safety.

// --- the errno reporter every `mk_libc_error(...)` expands to ---
extern fn mk_utils_libc_error(caller: ptr<mut, u8>, file: ptr<mut, u8>, line: i32) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(caller);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(file);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(line);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};
